  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM EDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nLarge surface high on the upwind/subsident side of deep eastern \nCanada trough will slide across our area today and ensure another \npleasant weather day. Expect just some passing high clouds and highs \naround 70F. There are some nonzero fire wx concerns today. Forecast \nsoundings show relatively deep mixing into a very dry airmass. \nSurface dewpoints likely crash into the upper 30s this afternoon \nyielding RH values around 30 percent. Surface gradient is not \nparticularly strong but deep mixing may yield some gusts up to 20 \nmph. These values are still safely below red flag criteria and ten \nhour fuel moistures are still above 10 percent but we often see a \nfew field/combine fires across the area in these conditions during \nharvest time.\n\nSurface high slides to the southeast tonight and return flow ramps \nup during the day Friday. Several solutions still show some light \nrain showers developing on the nose of the best LLJ/theta-e surge \nset to clip our far NW CWA Fri evening. Added some low chance PoP's \nto account for this but the low level moisture surge will be \nsignificantly impacted by antecedent dry conditions and any rain \namounts will be very light. SW flow/WAA will push highs well into \nthe 70s on Fri and to around 80F on Sat. Clouds increase late Sat \nbut dry conditions will hold through the daytime hours.\n\nAttention then turns to the remnants of Isaias still set to lift \ninto the Ohio Valley Sat night into Sunday. While exact details \nregarding the track and subsequent rainfall amounts in our CWA still \nshow some variability, there is increasing concensus that the \nheaviest rain will lift through central and eastern Ohio while our \nCWA only receives a glancing blow of light rain. The only area to \nwatch for some brief moderate rain will be our far SE around \nPortland to Lima but even there not expecting enough to cause any \nimpacts. The remnants of Isaias look to get quickly absorbed into \nthe mean westerlies with the best tropical moisture plume riding \nalong and east of the Appalachians. The main circulation weakens \nsubstantially by the time it reaches Ohio and even wind gusts look \nto remain below 30 mph. Will obviously continue to watch this period \nclosely but overall little/no impacts anticipated for our CWA.\n\nRain exits late in the day Sunday but another cold front arrives \nwith our next chance of rain Mon night. After one more day near 80F \non Monday, highs drop back into the 60s for the middle of next week \nwith lows well into the 40s.",
